    "lawCode": "Add §6348, CPLR; amd §§530.12 & 530.13, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7929\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules and the criminal proce-\ndure law, in relation to notifying certain individuals of their ability\nto apply for an extreme risk protection order\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRequires that anyone who applies for an order of protection from another\nperson, or is granted protection from another person by means of an\norder of protection, shall be notified of their ability to apply for an\nextreme risk protection order.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the civil practice law and rules to require that when-\never an order of protection is applied for, issued, modified, or\nextended under the family court act or the domestic relations law, the\ncourt must issue a notice regarding the ability to apply for an extreme\nrisk protection order.\n\nSections 2 and 3 amend sections 530.12 and 530.13 of the criminal proce-\ndure law to require that whenever an order of protection is applied for,\nissued, modified, or extended under such sections, respectively, the\ncourt must issue a notice regarding the ability to apply for an extreme\nrisk protection order.\n\nSection 4 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's \"Red Flag\" law is a landmark piece of legislation that makes\nNew Yorkers safer by allowing individuals who fear for their safety to\napply to have firearms removed from the possession of persons who may\npose a danger to themselves or others.\n\nHowever, the ability to apply for an extreme risk protection order\n(ERPO) is not always widely known. Instances have been reported when\nvictims and potential victims have not been aware of the option to\nobtain an ERPO when it would have benefited them. In one instance, a\nwoman obtained an order of protection against her husband and a number\nof guns were surrendered in connection with that order.  However, she\nfeared he had additional firearms in his possession, and she eventually\nwas proven right when she found them.\n\nShe later said she was unaware that she could have applied for an ERPO\nin addition to the order of protection already in effect which, if\ngranted, would have resulted in immediate additional action by law\nenforcement to search for and remove those additional firearms.  Certain\nother orders of protection provide for the suspension of a license to\npossess firearms and surrender of firearms in certain circumstances, but\nan ERPO has the added benefit of providing for the ability of law\nenforcement to proactively search for and remove such firearms.\n\nThis bill ensures that those who apply for an order of protection, or\nare granted one by the court without application, are made aware of\ntheir ability to avail themselves of an ERPO as well. This will broaden\nthe effect of the Red Flag law and ultimately save lives by informing\nvictims and potential victims of the additional protective tools avail-\nable.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThe act shall take effect 30 days after it shall have become a law.\n",
